Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book by Margery Williams
Draft date: 3/21/2025
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period children's fantasy; naturalistic faces, fabrics, fur, and nursery detail; one continuous live-action look for humans, toys, and animals.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Bright winter light fills an early-twentieth-century child's nursery. A bulging Christmas stocking hangs by the hearth. Wedged in the top sits the VELVETEEN RABBIT—fat and bunchy, spotted brown-and-white velveteen coat, real thread whiskers, ears lined with bright pink sateen, polished boot-button eyes, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: tissue paper rustling, distant cheerful voices)

THE BOY, a young child in simple Christmas-morning clothes, pulls the Rabbit free and hugs him hard for a long moment.

Nuts, oranges, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, and a clockwork mouse spill from the stocking. The Rabbit is quite the best of all.

Aunts and Uncles arrive in a rush of coats and parcels. Tissue paper flies. In the excitement of new presents the Velveteen Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor, forgotten.

(MUSIC: bright carol fragment fading under footsteps)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. Daylight. The Velveteen Rabbit—still mostly splendid, only a little less stiff—lives in the open toy cupboard or on the nursery floor. Mechanical toys stand superior. A model boat and TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, seem to look down on him. He is naturally shy.

(SOUND: clockwork whir, faint tin rattle of toys)

Near the nursery fender the SKIN HORSE lies—old brown coat bald in patches showing seams, most tail hairs pulled out. The Rabbit settles beside him.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

The Skin Horse's wise eyes hold the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, longing and a little sad at the thought of growing shabby.

(SOUND: Nana's distant footsteps approaching)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Night-light burns on the mantelpiece. NANA, in plain dark dress and white apron, hurries the bedtime routine. THE BOY in a simple nightshirt cannot find the china dog that always sleeps with him.

(SOUND: cupboard door creak, bedclothes rustle)

Nana sees the toy cupboard door standing open and makes a swoop. She drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts him into the Boy's arms. The Boy hugs him very tight. Sometimes he rolls over on him; sometimes he pushes him far under the pillow so the Rabbit can scarcely breathe.

Later, after Nana has gone to supper, the Boy whispers and makes nice tunnels under the bedclothes like burrows. When the Boy drops off, the Rabbit snuggles under the warm chin, the Boy's hands clasped close round him all night long.

(SOUND: soft breathing, night-light hiss)

Time passes in the same bed. The Rabbit's beautiful velveteen fur grows shabbier; his tail becomes unsewn; pink rubs off his nose where the Boy has kissed him. He is so happy he never notices.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

Sunlit garden. THE BOY in light outdoor play clothes gives the now-worn Velveteen Rabbit rides in a wheelbarrow, picnics on the grass, and builds lovely fairy huts under the raspberry canes behind the flower border. The Rabbit dives into burrows the Boy has made in the flower bed and comes up earthy.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow wheel squeak, distant tea-call)

One afternoon the Boy is called away suddenly to tea. The Rabbit is left out on the lawn until long after dusk. Dew soaks him.

NANA approaches with a candle, grumbling as she rubs him off with a corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

THE BOY sits up in bed in his nightshirt, arms stretched out. Nana still holds the damp Rabbit.

(SOUND: bed creak, candle snuffed)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The little Rabbit hears it. Love stirs in his sawdust heart. Into his boot-button eyes, long ago lost their polish, comes a look of wisdom and beauty.

NANA
(picking him up next morning)
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - SUMMER EVENING

Long June evening. Near the house a wood. THE BOY in play clothes carries the very shabby Velveteen Rabbit—whiskers mostly loved off, ear lining grey, spots fading, shape loosening—and makes him a little nest among the tall bracken before wandering off to pick flowers or play at brigands.

(SOUND: evening insects, soft wind in leaves)

The Rabbit lies alone watching ants run between his velvet paws. Two WILD RABBITS creep out of the tall bracken—quite furry, seams not showing, bodies changing shape as they move, noses twitching. They pad close.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The wild rabbit hops sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can! Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit has no hind legs at all; his back is made all in one piece like a pincushion. He sits still, hoping they will not notice.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The wild rabbit stretches his neck and looks.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

He laughs. The Velveteen Rabbit insists he is sitting on them. The wild rabbit whirls and dances until the stuffed one grows dizzy.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. THE BOY runs past. With a stamp of feet and a flash of white tails the two strange rabbits disappear into the bracken.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Only ants and swaying bracken. The Boy returns and carries him home.

(SOUND: fading footfalls, bracken fronds brushing)

Weeks pass. The Rabbit grows still older and more shapeless, yet to the Boy he is always beautiful. Shabbiness does not matter, because he is Real.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

THE BOY lies flushed and burning with fever, talking in his sleep. His little body is so hot it burns the Velveteen Rabbit held close. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night.

(SOUND: labored breathing, low adult murmurs, clock ticking)

The Rabbit stays hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring, afraid someone might take him away. He knows the Boy needs him. He whispers planned garden games into the half-sleeping ear. At last the fever turns. The Boy sits up with picture-books; the Rabbit cuddles close.

INT. NURSERY - BRIGHT MORNING

Windows stand wide open. Sunny air. THE BOY, pale but better, is carried out wrapped in a shawl.

EXT. BALCONY - DAY

THE BOY sits on the small balcony in the shawl, looking out. Inside, the Velveteen Rabbit lies tangled among the bedclothes, only his head peeping.

(SOUND: light breeze, distant garden birds)

Voices drift from the room—plans for the seaside tomorrow, disinfection, books and toys that were played with in bed to be burnt.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
(in dark suit, decisive)
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs!—Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

EXT. END OF THE GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

The little Rabbit is put into a sack with old picture-books and rubbish and carried to the end of the garden behind the fowl-house—a fine place for a bonfire. The gardener is busy with potatoes and peas; he will burn the lot in the morning. The sack is left untied.

(SOUND: distant spade, hens, then quiet)

That night THE BOY sleeps in a different bedroom with a splendid new white-plush bunny. He is too excited about the seaside to care much.

EXT. END OF THE GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Moonlight. The shabby Velveteen Rabbit has wriggled his head through the sack opening. He shivers; his coat is thin and threadbare. Nearby the raspberry canes stand tall like a jungle. He remembers sunlit hours, fairy huts, the wood, the day he knew he was Real, the wise Skin Horse.

(SOUND: night insects, faint rustle of canes)

A great sadness comes over him. Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his little shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

Where the tear fell, a mysterious flower grows—slender green leaves the colour of emeralds, a blossom like a golden cup. It opens. Out steps the NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY—dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers round her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

(MUSIC: soft luminous chime-like theme)

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am? I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She holds him close and flies with him into the wood.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moon risen. Fronds of bracken shine like frosted silver. In an open glade wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass. They stop and stand in a ring as the Fairy descends.

(SOUND: soft thumps of rabbit feet, night breeze)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits quite still, shy about his missing hind legs—then something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He has hind legs. Instead of dingy velveteen he has brown fur, soft and shiny. His ears twitch by themselves. His whiskers are so long they brush the grass.

(SOUND: sudden joyful thumps on turf)

He gives one leap. The joy of those hind legs sends him springing, jumping sideways, whirling as the others do. When at last he stops to look for the Fairy she has gone.

He is a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

Autumn and winter have passed. Warm sunny spring. THE BOY, healthy again in outdoor play clothes, plays in the wood behind the house.

(SOUND: birdsong, soft undergrowth rustle)

Two rabbits creep out from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through. About his little soft nose and round black eyes there is something familiar.

THE BOY
(softly, to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

He never knows that it really is his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who first helped him to be Real.

The spotted rabbit holds still a moment longer, then both slip back into the bracken.

(MUSIC: gentle resolving theme)

FADE OUT.

THE END

